# The PowerVR GX6250 is a render-only GPU (no display outputs), so its
# /dev/dri/card* node is a trap for any compositor that enumerates DRM cards
# and assumes each one can scan out. Clients that want the GPU open the render
# node (/dev/dri/renderD128) directly, so hiding the card node costs nothing.
#
# MUTTER (GNOME, gnome-mobile): adopting the card node as a secondary GPU
# makes its cogl buffer handling start failing (cogl_buffer_impl_gl_map_range
# assertions, "Failed to lock front buffer on /dev/dri/card1"), and the next
# screen fade kills the frame clock ("clutter_frame_clock_notify_ready: code
# should not be reached"), leaving the panel permanently black with the
# backlight off. Verified on hardware 2026-08-30: the first cogl failure
# landed five seconds after the powervr driver bound. mutter-mobile checks the
# udev TAG "mutter-device-ignore" -- its hotplug path never reads the
# MUTTER_DEVICE_IGNORE property, learned the hard way: a mutter already
# running when the GPU binds would try to adopt card0, fail, corrupt its EGL
# context, and die at the next screen fade. Set both forms.
#
# KWIN (Plasma, Plasma Mobile): NOT covered here, and not yet tested on this
# board. KWin has no udev opt-out; the tags below are simply ignored by it.
# If Plasma turns out to trip over the render-only card the same way, the knob
# is KWIN_DRM_DEVICES, a colon-separated list of the devices KWin may use.
# Do not hardcode a card number for it: which of the two DRM devices ends up
# as card0 is not stable on this board (mediatek-drm and powervr both bind
# from modules, powervr only at ~18 s), so resolve the display device at
# runtime -- e.g. the /dev/dri/by-path symlink of the mediatek display
# controller -- rather than writing /dev/dri/card0 into a unit.
SUBSYSTEM=="drm", KERNEL=="card*", DRIVERS=="powervr", TAG+="mutter-device-ignore", ENV{MUTTER_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1"
